# Built plugin binaries. The Go plugin's entry is a compiled executable, so it
# is a build output like any other and never travels in the repository.
go/bin/
go/gocraft-example-go
go/gocraft-example-go.exe

# `go build ./go/` names the binary after the directory, and the directory is
# called go. Only the Windows form needs a rule: elsewhere the name collides
# with the directory and the build refuses rather than writing it. Anchored,
# and never `/go` — that would ignore the plugin itself.
/go.exe

# What `-gocraft-dump-commands` writes. The bundle build reads it and the packer
# skips it, which is why it can live inside the plugin directory at all.
go/.gocraft/

# Gradle.
java/.gradle/
java/build/

# Bundles. They are what this repository produces, and the test server takes a
# copy; neither belongs in the history.
*.gcpkg
# What `gocraft-cli gen` writes. Generated from the other plugin's manifest at
# build time, so committing it would be committing a copy of somebody else's
# description — the very thing generating it exists to avoid.
go/internal/
java/src/main/java/gocraft/example/greeting/
